<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Hatra</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 241 CE (approx.) · capital Hatra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A fortified caravan city in the desert of northern Iraq, ruled by 'kings of the Arabs', that twice beat off Roman emperors before falling to the Sasanians.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hatra grew in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Jazira</a> steppe west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> as a religious and caravan centre in the 1st century BCE under Parthian protection. Its rulers at first bore the title 'lord' (mry'), and from the mid-2nd century CE, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanatruq-i">Sanatruq I</a>, they styled themselves 'king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>'.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>At its centre stood a vast temple precinct to the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>, called Maran ('our lord'), with shrines to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allat">Allat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalshamin">Baalshamin</a> and other deities. Hundreds of inscriptions in Hatran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and statues of kings, nobles and priests reveal a society blending Arab, Aramaean, Mesopotamian and Parthian traditions. Its round double walls withstood sieges by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> in 117 CE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> in 198 to 199.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hatra allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> but was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> around 240 to 241 and abandoned. Its ruins, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, were damaged by Islamic State fighters in 2015.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-hatra.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Hatra" width="320" height="290"><figcaption>Image: IchthyovenatorSémhur (base map) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_Mesopotamian_vassal_kingdoms_AD_200.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 241 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hatra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sanatruq I, Abdsamiya, Sanatruq II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Arab and Mesopotamian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hatra, Kingdom of Araba</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=70&amp;focus=kingdom-of-hatra" aria-label="Kingdom of Hatra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5759 892 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Hatra lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6079 1148L6141 1232M6079 1148L5304 942M6079 1148L6357 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6141 1232h0M5304 942h0M6357 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6079 1148h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6079 1148h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hatra. <a href="/atlas?year=70&amp;focus=kingdom-of-hatra">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

